Below is a **sample** of what the JSON payload would look like after filtering the full VirusTotal response to keep only the `id` field and any `context_attributes`.  
(Actual values will differ when you run the request against the live API.)

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": {
          "harmless": 12,
          "malicious": 0,
          "suspicious": 0,
          "undetected": 5,
          "timeout": 0
        },
        "last_analysis_date": 1711234567,
        "last_analysis_results": { /* omitted */ }
      },
      "relationships": { /* omitted */ }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": {
          "harmless": 9,
          "malicious": 1,
          "suspicious": 0,
          "undetected": 8,
          "timeout": 0
        },
        "last_analysis_date": 1711237890,
        "last_analysis_results": { /* omitted */ }
      },
      "relationships": { /* omitted */ }
    }
    /* … up to 10 items, truncated for brevity … */
  ],
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "defghijklmnop"
  }
}
```

### How to make the request

```bash
curl -X GET \
     "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/objects/domain/example.org/relationships?relationship_type=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc" \
     -H "x-apikey: my_api" \
     -H "Accept: application/json"
```

> **Note:**  
> * Replace `my_api` with your actual VirusTotal API key.  
> * The `continuation_cursor` value (`abc`) is used to paginate results; the API will return a new cursor in the `meta.continuation_cursor` field if more pages are available.  

Once you receive the raw JSON from the API, you can programmatically strip out everything except the `id` and any `context_attributes` (if present) using a script in your preferred language.
